, 70.206, 70.264, 70.334, 70.392, 70.461, 70.531, 70.705, 70.984, 71.146]}, "text": "So how do we know any of this?\n\nNone of this was written down when the poems were first made.\n\nFor generations it was recited from memory by poets.\n\nMost of the poems survive in one great book, the Codex Regius, from about twelve seventy.\n\nIt was written in Iceland, by Christians, about gods nobody worshipped anymore.\n\nAround twelve twenty, an Icelandic chieftain named Snorry Sturloosun put together a handbook for poets.\n\nSnorri was a Christian too, and his book opens with Adam and Eve, then says the gods were once men from Asia.\n\nNot every source agrees.\n\nIn one poem, the makers of the first people have different names.\n\nWhen they disagree, we follow Snorri.\n\nHe was killed in twelve forty-one, on the orders of a Norwegian king.\n\nSome of the gods barely got a mention here.\n\nFraya and Fray. Eedoon and her apples.\n\nA giant who stole Thor's hammer, and a war between two families of gods.\n\nFollow the channel for part two."}